<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SActionScript version="1" name="meter_gain_after_probe">

  <!-- Proposal 37 P3a, AC3: A FADER CHANGE MADE AFTER A POSITION WAS ALREADY
       FROZEN IS OBSERVED BY assert-meter's PATH.

       This case could not be written before P3a, and its absence is why
       meter_postfader.qxa uses two tracks at two gains rather than one track
       whose gain moves. The caveat it worked around, recorded in
       smetertestactions.cpp and in CLAUDE.md's "Level meters" section:

           the fader lived in twTrackMix, and the cached page between the chain
           and the rewire was gated on the CHAIN's content epoch, so a gain set
           after that position had been frozen once was not picked up here -
           even though playback and the offline render, which go through the
           scheduler and re-plan, both saw it.

       The fader is now twGainStage, and the rewire's producer IS the gain
       stage, so the epoch the cached page is gated on is the epoch
       set-track-volume bumps. Hence: probe first, THEN set the gain, then probe
       the SAME position again and require the new level.

       Fixture: ../test_sawtooth.wav, whose 4th second has rms ~0.405
       (testkit/CONTRACT.md inv. 3); position 168000 is inside it, and the
       256-frame probe window ending there has rms 0.414548. The two bands are
       meter_postfader.qxa's, unchanged, so the two cases are directly
       comparable: 0.300-0.510 at unity, 0.150-0.255 at -6.02 dB. They do not
       overlap, so "the second probe returned the first probe's number" fails. -->

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- FIRST probe: freezes the page for this position at unity. -->
    <assert-meter trackIndex="0" position="168000" minRms="0.300" maxRms="0.510"/>

    <!-- The gain change comes AFTER that freeze. This is the whole case. -->
    <set-track-volume trackIndex="0" volume="-6.0206"/>

    <assert-meter trackIndex="0" position="168000" minRms="0.150" maxRms="0.255"/>

    <!-- And back up again, so the direction is not what makes it work. -->
    <set-track-volume trackIndex="0" volume="0"/>

    <assert-meter trackIndex="0" position="168000" minRms="0.300" maxRms="0.510"/>

    <!-- An undo must be audible to the meter too. NOTE WHAT IT UNDOES: the two
         set-track-volume actions above have the same mergeKey (the lane path),
         so the action system COALESCED them into one undo step whose inverse
         carries the volume from before the FIRST of them. One undo therefore
         lands on the project's original 0 dB, not on -6.02 dB - which is the
         correct merge behaviour, not a bug, and is why nothing here asserts a
         per-action undo. What is being gated is only that the inverse reaches
         the audio at a position that has been frozen three times already. -->
    <undo count="1"/>

    <assert-meter trackIndex="0" position="168000" minRms="0.300" maxRms="0.510"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
